course: dictionary
lesson: 4304
title: Grip: hold, slip and feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Grip is a word with several meanings. As a verb, to grip is to hold something very tightly. As a noun, a grip is a tight hold, or the part of something you hold, like a racket handle. The context tells you which meaning fits: «Keep a firm grip on the rope» is about holding, while «Shoes need good grip» is about not slipping.
explain: Grip can also describe how a surface holds on. Grip is how well something holds onto a surface without slipping. «The grooves in a tyre's tread push water away so the rubber can grip» shows that the tread helps the tyre hold the road. Wet and icy surfaces have less grip, so cars need longer to stop.
explain: Grip can even describe a story. «The story gripped me» means the story held your attention very strongly, so you could not stop reading. To check a meaning, swap in a simpler word, like hold, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Grip can be a verb or a noun: you can grip a bat, or get a good grip on it.
fact: «Cat claws curve backwards, so they grip well going up but not going down head first.»
fact: Rough soles and rubber handles grip better because friction helps them hold on.
fact: «Get a grip» is a phrase that means calm down.
fact: The UK minimum tread depth for cars is 1.6 millimetres.
exercise: «Keep a firm grip on the rope.» What does grip mean here? | *A tight hold | A type of knot | A loud noise | A long walk
exercise: «Shoes need good grip.» In this sentence, what does grip mean? | How fast shoes can run | The size of the shoes | *How well shoes hold onto the ground without slipping | The colour of the laces
exercise: «The story gripped me.» What does gripped mean here? | Made me hungry | *Held my attention very strongly | Held my hand tightly | Made me fall asleep
test: «Wet roads have less grip, so cars need much longer to stop.» Why do cars need longer to stop on wet roads? | Because wet roads are shorter | Because the rain makes cars lighter | Because cars cannot use their brakes at all | *Because the tyres slip more and hold on less
test: Which phrase means calm down? | In the grip of | A firm grip | Lose your grip | *Get a grip
exam: Ella says grip is only a noun. Is she right? | No, grip is only ever a colour | Yes, grip is only ever a verb | Yes, grip is only ever a noun | *No, grip can be a verb too, as in «grip the bat»
